var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-overview","page":"Overview","text":"The package is an umbrella module (GBGIsotropicTurbulence) assembled from submodules, in dependency order: SimulationState → Equations → Forcing → TimeIntegration. The umbrella re-exports their public surface, so using GBGIsotropicTurbulence brings everything into scope.\n\nGBGIsotropicTurbulence: the top-level module and public re-export surface.\nSimulationState: the spectral machinery (SpectralOperators), the preallocated buffers (VelocityWorkspace), the scratch allocators, and the random divergence-free initial condition.\nEquations: the rotational-form Navier–Stokes right-hand side (the de-aliased, Leray-projected nonlinear term and the exact viscous integrating factor) and the velocity diagnostics.\nForcing: the pluggable energy-injection strategies under AbstractForcing (NoForcing, BandRescaling, Linear, ConstantPower).\nTimeIntegration: the pluggable integrating-factor time-stepping schemes under AbstractIntegrator (IFRK2).\n\nPages = [\n  \"api/GBGIsotropicTurbulence.md\",\n  \"api/SimulationState.md\",\n  \"api/Equations.md\",\n  \"api/Forcing.md\",\n  \"api/TimeIntegration.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"API overview"},{"category":"section","location":"api/GBGIsotropicTurbulence/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence","page":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence","text":"The umbrella module: it sets the include order and re-exports the public surface of the submodules.","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/GBGIsotropicTurbulence/#Index","page":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence","text":"Modules = [GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/GBGIsotropicTurbulence/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.GBGIsotropicTurbulence","page":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence","text":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence\n\nTools for simulating and analysing homogeneous isotropic turbulence on three-dimensional periodic domains: a velocity field and a single passive scalar varphi, each optionally forced, advanced by a choice of integrating-factor time-stepping scheme, with Lagrangian particle tracking and Eulerian + Lagrangian statistics.\n\nBuilt on GBGPeriodicFields.\n\nThe GBG part in the name comes from the city of Göteborg (Gothenburg, Sweden), where the primary development took place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.GBGIsotropicTurbulence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#TimeIntegration","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"","title":"TimeIntegration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#Public-API","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"TimeIntegration\n\nThe pluggable time-marching schemes, as concrete AbstractIntegrator subtypes dispatched by step!, which advances the coupled velocity + passive-scalar state. All are of the integrating-factor family: the stiff diffusive part is advanced exactly and the nonlinear term explicitly by a Runge–Kutta stage combination. The default is IFLowStorageRK3; recommended_cfl gives a scheme's advective-CFL ceiling; Physics bundles the problem parameters and forcings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.AbstractIntegrator","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"AbstractIntegrator\n\nSupertype of the time-marching schemes. A scheme implements step!(integrator, uh, phih, ops, ws, physics, dt), advancing the velocity uh and the scalar phih (both in spectral representation) by one timestep in place, and may specialize recommended_cfl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.AbstractIntegrator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.IFLowStorageRK3","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"IFLowStorageRK3()\n\nIntegrating-factor third-order low-storage Runge–Kutta (Williamson 2N). With the per-substep integrating factor mathcalE_D(tau) = exp(-Dlvertmathbfkrvert^2tau) (D = nu for the velocity, kappa = numathrmSc for the scalar) and widehatmathbfN the de-aliased nonlinearity of each field, the step carries a state hat q and a single accumulator mathbfh through three stages i = 123,\n\nmathbfh leftarrow A_imathbfh + Delta twidehatmathbfN(hat q)\nqquad\nhat q leftarrow hat q + B_imathbfh\nqquad\nhat q leftarrow mathcalE_D(d_iDelta t)hat q\nquad\nmathbfh leftarrow mathcalE_D(d_iDelta t)mathbfh\n\nwith A = (0 -59 -153128), B = (13 1516 815), and node increments d = (13 512 14) (abscissae c = (0 13 34)). The velocity and scalar are advanced in lockstep (the same ABd), each with its own diffusivity; setting D = 0 recovers the bare Williamson scheme, and dropping the nonlinearity recovers exact diffusion mathcalE_D(Delta t). Three nonlinear evaluations per step, two field registers per field — the default workhorse: third-order and stable on the imaginary axis (limit sqrt3), unlike IFRK2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.IFLowStorageRK3"},{"category":"type","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.IFRK2","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"IFRK2()\n\nIntegrating-factor second-order Runge–Kutta (Heun). With the integrating factors mathcalE_nu = exp(-nulvertmathbfkrvert^2Delta t) and mathcalE_kappa = exp(-kappalvertmathbfkrvert^2Delta t) and widehatmathbfN each field's de-aliased nonlinearity, one step of a field hat q with integrating factor mathcalE is\n\nhat q^* = mathcalE(hat q_n + Delta twidehatmathbfN_1)\nqquad\nhat q_n+1 = mathcalEhat q_n + tfracDelta t2big(mathcalEwidehatmathbfN_1 + widehatmathbfN_2big)\n\nwith widehatmathbfN_1 at hat q_n and widehatmathbfN_2 at the predictor hat q^*; velocity and scalar use mathcalE_nu and mathcalE_kappa respectively, then the forcings are applied. Two nonlinear evaluations per step.\n\nnote: Note\nEvery two-stage explicit Runge–Kutta has stability function R(z) = 1 + z + z^22, for which lvert R(iy)rvert^2 = 1 + y^44  1: the scheme is unstable on the imaginary axis, so it is stable only where the viscous damping of the integrating factor dominates. It is adequate for well-resolved, modest-Reynolds-number runs but is not the default — prefer IFLowStorageRK3 for high Reynolds number or marginal resolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.IFRK2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.Physics","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"Physics(; viscosity, schmidt = 1.0, velocity_forcing = NoForcing(), scalar_forcing = NoForcing())\n\nThe problem definition consumed by step!: the kinematic viscosity nu (viscosity), the Schmidt number mathrmSc (schmidt; the scalar diffusivity is kappa = numathrmSc), and the velocity and scalar forcing strategies (velocity_forcing, scalar_forcing, both AbstractForcing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.Physics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.default_integrator-Tuple{}","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"default_integrator()\n\nThe recommended default scheme, IFLowStorageRK3 — third-order, low-storage, and stable on the imaginary axis (unlike IFRK2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.default_integrator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.recommended_cfl-Tuple{AbstractIntegrator}","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"recommended_cfl(integrator)\n\nA conservative advective-CFL ceiling C for the scheme, for a timestep Delta t = CDelta x  max_ilvert u_irvert.\n\nThe advective eigenvalues of the de-aliased system are imaginary, of magnitude up to k_maxmaxlvertmathbfurvert with k_max = tfrac23tfracN2tfrac2piL, so k_maxDelta x = 2pi3 approx 209; stability needs this times C to stay within the scheme's imaginary-axis stability limit y_mathrms. For IFLowStorageRK3 y_mathrms = sqrt3 approx 173 (so C lesssim 083), giving the conservative 05; IFRK2 has y_mathrms = 0 (imaginary-axis unstable, relying on viscous damping), so a small 025. These are defaults — override for a specific run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.recommended_cfl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TimeIntegration/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.step!","page":"TimeIntegration","text":"step!(integrator, uh, phih, ops, ws, physics, dt)\n\nAdvance the spectral velocity uh and passive scalar phih by one timestep dt in place, using the scheme integrator, the operators ops, the workspace ws, and the problem physics (a Physics: viscosity, Schmidt number, and the two forcings). Dispatches on the concrete integrator type (see IFRK2, IFLowStorageRK3). The caller must have filled ws.velocity and ws.vorticity_start with the start-of-step physical velocity and vorticity (via velocity_vorticity!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.TimeIntegration.step!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Forcing/#Forcing","page":"Forcing","text":"","title":"Forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Forcing/#Public-API","page":"Forcing","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing","page":"Forcing","text":"Forcing\n\nThe pluggable energy-injection strategies, concrete AbstractForcing subtypes. A strategy hooks into the step through forcing_rate! (a linear rate folded into the integrating factor) and/or apply_forcing! (a post-step projection). NoForcing, BandRescaling, Linear, and ConstantPower are provided; one implementation serves both the velocity and the scalar varphi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.AbstractForcing","page":"Forcing","text":"AbstractForcing\n\nSupertype of the energy-injection strategies. A strategy may implement forcing_rate! (a linear forcing rate folded into the integrating factor) and/or apply_forcing! (a post-step projection); both default to doing nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.AbstractForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.BandRescaling","page":"Forcing","text":"BandRescaling(ops; energy, lowest_shell = 1, highest_shell = 2)\n\nDeterministic constant-energy forcing (post-step). Each step the in-band Fourier modes (textttlowest_shell le lvertmathbfkrvert le texttthighest_shell, in integer shells of 2piL) are rescaled by a single factor so the band holds the fixed energy tfrac12langlelvertcdotrvert^2rangle_mathrmband = textttenergy (band kinetic energy for the velocity, band variance for the scalar varphi). Rescaling preserves the divergence-free constraint. It fixes the band energy, not the injection rate, so the total energy oscillates slowly about its mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.BandRescaling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.ConstantPower","page":"Forcing","text":"ConstantPower(ops; power, lowest_shell = 1, highest_shell = 2)\n\nConstant-power forcing: a banded linear forcing f = Amathbfu whose rate is set each step so the energy-injection rate langlemathbfucdot frangle = A(2E_mathrmband) equals the target varepsilon = textttpower — i.e. A = varepsilon  (2E_mathrmband) with E_mathrmband = tfrac12langlelvertcdotrvert^2rangle_mathrmband of the start-of-step field. The rate is folded into the integrating factor; like Linear, it preserves a solenoidal velocity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.ConstantPower"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.Linear","page":"Forcing","text":"Linear(ops; rate, lowest_shell = 1, highest_shell = 2)\n\nLinear (negative-damping) forcing f = Amathbfu in a low-wavenumber band (Lundgren-type), with the fixed rate A = textttrate on shells textttlowest_shell le lvertmathbfkrvert le texttthighest_shell. The rate is folded into the integrating factor (exact for the linear term), so the forced band modes grow at net rate A - nulvertmathbfkrvert^2. A positive rate injects energy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.Linear"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.NoForcing","page":"Forcing","text":"NoForcing()\n\nThe unforced case: both hooks do nothing (a freely decaying field).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.NoForcing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.apply_forcing!-Tuple{AbstractForcing, Any, SpectralOperators, Any}","page":"Forcing","text":"apply_forcing!(forcing, field, ops, scratch) -> field\n\nApply a post-step forcing to field (a velocity or scalar varphi field in spectral representation) in place, using the spectral scratch buffer. The default does nothing; see BandRescaling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.apply_forcing!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.forcing_rate!-Tuple{AbstractForcing, Any, Any, SpectralOperators, Any}","page":"Forcing","text":"forcing_rate!(forcing, rate, field, ops, ws) -> Bool\n\nFor a forcing linear in the field, fill the real per-mode rate array rate with A(mathbfk) (the forcing f = Atextttfield) and return true; the integrator folds it into the integrating factor as expbig((A(mathbfk) - Dlvertmathbfkrvert^2)taubig). Return false (the default) when the forcing contributes no linear rate. field is the start-of-step field in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.forcing_rate!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationState/#SimulationState","page":"SimulationState","text":"","title":"SimulationState"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationState/#Public-API","page":"SimulationState","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState","page":"SimulationState","text":"SimulationState\n\nThe spectral machinery and preallocated buffers a run advances: SpectralOperators (the read-only grid, transform plans, multipliers, squared-wavenumber field, and de-alias kernel), the scratch allocators, VelocityWorkspace, and the random divergence-free initial condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.SpectralOperators","page":"SimulationState","text":"SpectralOperators(resolution, box = 2π; dealias_fraction = 2//3,\n                  scalar_type = Float64, array_type = Array)\n\nThe reusable, read-only spectral machinery for a cubic homogeneous-isotropic-turbulence run on a grid of resolution points per side, in a periodic box of side box: the Grid3D, the real-to-complex transform plans (one for the velocity, a vector field; one for the scalar varphi), the FieldSpectralMultipliers, the squared-wavenumber array lvertmathbfkrvert^2 (with mathbfk the wavenumber vector — the symbol of -nabla^2), and the de-alias kernel dealias.\n\ndealias is an isotropic sharp low-pass that keeps the modes lvertmathbfkrvert le textttdealias_fractioncdot k_max (k_max the Nyquist wavenumber) — the 23 rule (dealias_fraction = 2//3) that removes the aliasing error of the quadratic nonlinearity.\n\narray_type selects the storage backend (Array for CPU, ROCArray/CuArray for GPU) and scalar_type the real element type (Float32 is GPU-friendly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.SpectralOperators"},{"category":"type","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.Workspace","page":"SimulationState","text":"Workspace(ops)\n\nPreallocated scratch for one coupled integrating-factor step of the velocity and the passive scalar varphi. The velocity buffers are the spectral vorticity, two Runge–Kutta stage registers, the predictor field, a velocity copy (the in-place inverse transform consumes its source), a general spectral scratch, the nonlinear-term buffer, and the physical velocity / vorticity / predictor / Lamb-vector (mathbfutimesboldsymbolomega). The scalar buffers are two stage registers, a predictor, the nonlinear-term buffer, a copy, the shared integrating factor, and the physical scalar; the scalar flux mathbfuvarphi reuses the physical and spectral velocity scratch. Sized so a step allocates no field-sized memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.Workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.dealias!-Tuple{Any, SpectralOperators}","page":"SimulationState","text":"dealias!(field, ops)\n\nApply the 23-rule low-pass ops.dealias to field in place (a coefficientwise, alias-safe multiply by the de-alias kernel; field may be in vector or scalar spectral representation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.dealias!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.physical_vector-Tuple{SpectralOperators}","page":"SimulationState","text":"physical_vector(ops)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed vector field in physical representation on ops's backend and element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.physical_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.random_scalar!-Tuple{Any, SpectralOperators}","page":"SimulationState","text":"random_scalar!(phih, ops; variance = 1.0, peak_wavenumber = 2.0, rng = default_rng())\n\nFill phih (the scalar varphi in spectral representation) with a random, mean-zero scalar concentrated at large scales by the envelope expbig(-lvertmathbfkrvert^2k_p^2big) (k_p is peak_wavenumber), de-aliased, and rescaled so the variance langlevarphi^2rangle equals variance. The physical noise is made mean-zero before the transform, so the mathbfk=mathbf 0 (mean) mode vanishes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.random_scalar!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.random_solenoidal_velocity!-Tuple{Any, SpectralOperators}","page":"SimulationState","text":"random_solenoidal_velocity!(uh, ops; energy = 0.5, peak_wavenumber = 2.0, rng = default_rng())\n\nFill uh (a velocity field in spectral representation) with a random, divergence-free velocity mathbfu concentrated at large scales by the envelope expbig(-lvertmathbfkrvert^2k_p^2big) (k_p is peak_wavenumber), de-aliased, and rescaled so the kinetic energy tfrac12langlelvertmathbfurvert^2rangle equals energy. The Leray–Helmholtz projection mathbbP enforces nablacdotmathbfu = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.random_solenoidal_velocity!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.scalar_physical-Tuple{SpectralOperators}","page":"SimulationState","text":"scalar_physical(ops)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed scalar field in physical representation on ops's backend and element type (used for the scalar varphi in physical space).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.scalar_physical"},{"category":"method","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.scalar_spectral-Tuple{SpectralOperators}","page":"SimulationState","text":"scalar_spectral(ops)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed scalar field in spectral representation on ops's backend and element type (used for the scalar varphi, the integrating factor, and kernel scratch).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.scalar_spectral"},{"category":"method","location":"api/SimulationState/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.spectral_vector-Tuple{SpectralOperators}","page":"SimulationState","text":"spectral_vector(ops)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed vector field in spectral representation on ops's backend and element type (a scratch buffer for the time loop).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationState.spectral_vector"},{"category":"section","location":"#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl","page":"Home","text":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl is a small, focused pseudospectral direct-numerical-simulation (DNS) package for homogeneous isotropic turbulence on a three-dimensional periodic domain: an incompressible velocity field mathbfu together with a single passive scalar varphi, each optionally forced, advanced by a choice of integrating-factor time-stepping scheme, with Lagrangian particle tracking and Eulerian + Lagrangian statistics.\n\nIt is built on GBGPeriodicFields, which supplies every field operation — transforms, spectral differential operators, convolution, interpolation, and reductions. GBGPeriodicFields is deliberately time- and particle-free; this package is the consumer that owns time integration, forcing, the scalar transport, particle tracking, and the statistics. The GPU is the primary target, so the time loop is in-place and allocation-free over a preallocated workspace.","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"This is a private, unregistered package. With repository access:\n\nusing Pkg\n\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/GrigorySarnitsky/GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl\")\n\nThen:\n\nusing GBGIsotropicTurbulence","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-it-solves","page":"Home","text":"With mathbfu the velocity, boldsymbolomega = nablatimesmathbfu the vorticity, nu the kinematic viscosity, mathbbP the Leray–Helmholtz projection onto divergence-free fields, and mathbff a forcing, the momentum equation is advanced in rotational form,\n\npartial_t mathbfu = mathbbP(mathbfutimesboldsymbolomega) + nunabla^2mathbfu + mathbff\nqquad nablacdotmathbfu = 0\n\nand the passive scalar varphi by the advection–diffusion equation\n\npartial_t varphi = -nablacdot(mathbfuvarphi) + fracnumathrmScnabla^2varphi + f_varphi\n\nwith mathrmSc the Schmidt number. In both equations the diffusive (stiff) term is integrated exactly through an integrating factor, the nonlinear term explicitly by a Runge–Kutta stage combination, and the quadratic product is de-aliased by the 23 rule.\n\nnote: Work in progress\nThe package is being built in vertical slices. The coupled velocity + passive-scalar solver is in place, advanced by the integrating-factor schemes (IFLowStorageRK3 — the default — and IFRK2), with the deterministic forcings NoForcing, BandRescaling, Linear, and ConstantPower (the stochastic Ornstein–Uhlenbeck forcing, Lagrangian particle tracking, and the statistics are being added).","title":"What it solves"},{"category":"section","location":"#Components","page":"Home","text":"SimulationState: the reusable spectral machinery (SpectralOperators), the preallocated buffers (VelocityWorkspace), and the random divergence-free initial condition.\nEquations: the rotational-form Navier–Stokes right-hand side and the velocity diagnostics (kinetic_energy, dissipation, max_speed).\nForcing: the pluggable energy-injection strategies under AbstractForcing.\nTimeIntegration: the pluggable integrating-factor schemes under AbstractIntegrator.","title":"Components"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Equations/#Equations","page":"Equations","text":"","title":"Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Equations/#Public-API","page":"Equations","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations","page":"Equations","text":"Equations\n\nThe incompressible Navier–Stokes right-hand side in rotational form — the de-aliased, Leray-projected nonlinear term and the exact viscous integrating factor — together with the velocity diagnostics (kinetic energy, dissipation, maximum speed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.dissipation-Tuple{Any, Real}","page":"Equations","text":"dissipation(vorticity, viscosity)\n\nThe mean kinetic-energy dissipation rate of a homogeneous flow, varepsilon = nulanglelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle, from the physical vorticity boldsymbolomega and the kinematic viscosity nu (the homogeneous identity varepsilon = 2nulangle S_ijS_ijrangle = nulanglelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.dissipation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.integrating_factor!","page":"Equations","text":"integrating_factor!(factor, ops, diffusivity, dt, rate = nothing)\n\nWrite the exact diffusive integrating factor into the scalar spectral buffer factor. Over a step Delta t the diffusion equation partial_t f = Dnabla^2 f is solved exactly in spectral representation by the per-mode multiplier\n\nmathcalE(mathbfk) = expbig(-Dlvertmathbfkrvert^2Delta tbig)\n\nso multiplying a field by factor advances its viscous/diffusive part exactly (no stability restriction). The diffusivity D (diffusivity) is the kinematic viscosity nu for the velocity and numathrmSc for the scalar varphi (mathrmSc the Schmidt number).\n\nWhen a linear forcing rate A(mathbfk) is supplied (rate, a real array; from the forcing_rate! of a Linear/ConstantPower forcing), it is folded into the linear operator, mathcalE(mathbfk) = expbig((A(mathbfk) - Dlvertmathbfkrvert^2)Delta tbig) — exact for the linear forcing, so the integrator keeps its order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.integrating_factor!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.kinetic_energy-Tuple{Any}","page":"Equations","text":"kinetic_energy(velocity)\n\nThe turbulent kinetic energy tfrac12langlelvertmathbfurvert^2rangle of the velocity field velocity. Computed as tfrac12langlemathbfumathbfurangle with the normalized inner product, so by Parseval's theorem it accepts velocity in either representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.kinetic_energy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.max_speed-Tuple{Any}","page":"Equations","text":"max_speed(velocity)\n\nThe largest velocity component in absolute value, max_ilvert u_irvert — an allocation-free reduction used to set the advective CFL timestep. It differs from the true suplvertmathbfurvert by at most sqrt3, well inside the CFL margin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.max_speed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.scalar_dissipation-Tuple{Any, Real}","page":"Equations","text":"scalar_dissipation(scalar_gradient, diffusivity)\n\nThe mean scalar-variance dissipation rate chi = 2kappalanglelvertnablavarphirvert^2rangle, from the physical scalar gradient scalar_gradient nablavarphi and the scalar diffusivity kappa = numathrmSc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.scalar_dissipation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.scalar_nonlinear!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, SpectralOperators, Workspace}","page":"Equations","text":"scalar_nonlinear!(nonlinear, velocity, scalar, ops, ws)\n\nAssemble the de-aliased advection term of the passive-scalar equation into nonlinear, from the physical velocity mathbfu and physical scalar varphi.\n\nIn conservative form the advection is -nablacdot(mathbfuvarphi) (equal to -mathbfucdotnablavarphi since nablacdotmathbfu = 0); the conservative form is used because, with the 23 rule, it conserves the scalar mean exactly (the divergence has no mathbfk=mathbf 0 component). It is computed pseudospectrally as -mathrmdealiasbig(imathbfkcdotwidehatmathbfuvarphibig). The scalar flux mathbfuvarphi reuses the physical and spectral velocity scratch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.scalar_nonlinear!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.scalar_to_physical!-Tuple{Any, Any, SpectralOperators, Workspace}","page":"Equations","text":"scalar_to_physical!(scalar, phih, ops, ws)\n\nCompute the physical scalar scalar varphi from phih (the scalar in spectral representation hatvarphi), preserving phih — a copy is transformed, since the in-place inverse transform consumes its source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.scalar_to_physical!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.scalar_variance-Tuple{Any}","page":"Equations","text":"scalar_variance(scalar)\n\nThe scalar variance langlevarphi^2rangle of the scalar field scalar (for a mean-zero varphi). Computed as langlevarphivarphirangle with the normalized inner product, so by Parseval's theorem it accepts scalar in either representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.scalar_variance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.velocity_nonlinear!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, SpectralOperators, Workspace}","page":"Equations","text":"velocity_nonlinear!(nonlinear, velocity, vorticity, ops, ws)\n\nAssemble the de-aliased, Leray-projected nonlinear term of the momentum equation into nonlinear, from the physical velocity mathbfu and vorticity boldsymbolomega.\n\nIn physical space the rotational form writes the convective term as the Lamb vector: with the identity (mathbfucdotnabla)mathbfu = nablabig(tfrac12lvertmathbfurvert^2big) - mathbfutimesboldsymbolomega, the divergence-free momentum equation becomes partial_tmathbfu = mathbbP(mathbfutimesboldsymbolomega) + nunabla^2mathbfu + mathbff, where the Leray–Helmholtz projection mathbbP onto divergence-free fields absorbs the pressure and the nabla(tfrac12lvertmathbfurvert^2) gradient. The term computed here is the spectral image of mathbbP(mathbfutimesboldsymbolomega), with the 23 rule removing the aliasing of the quadratic product. Allocation-free (a destination distinct from its sources takes the no-alias path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.velocity_nonlinear!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Equations/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.velocity_vorticity!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, SpectralOperators, Workspace}","page":"Equations","text":"velocity_vorticity!(velocity, vorticity, uh, ops, ws)\n\nCompute the physical velocity mathbfu and vorticity boldsymbolomega = nablatimesmathbfu of the velocity field uh (given in spectral representation hatmathbfu) into the buffers velocity and vorticity.\n\nuh is preserved: the vorticity is transformed first, then a copy of uh is transformed — the in-place inverse transform consumes its spectral source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Equations.velocity_vorticity!"}]
}
